WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Let’s talk about Alexey Navalny, the man Russian President Vladimir Putin won’t mention by name.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.680
Depending on who you are in Russia, Navalny’s a brave anti-corruption campaigner standing up to Putin and his comrades.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.760
Or he’s a Western stooge making trouble.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.680
He’s been arrested, jailed, even poisoned — and now he’s back behind bars.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:41.440
It’s why thousands of Russians have been protesting and getting arrested.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.320
So who is Alexey Navalny? What’s his game plan? And should Putin be worried?

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:03.040
Russia is Putin’s world. And everyone’s just living in it. They kind of have to.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:11.840
He controls basically everything: the state media, state-run companies, a powerful intelligence network and his critics say the justice system too.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.040
Putin’s had his political challengers.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.360
But no one’s really got the kind of attention that Alexey Navalny has.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.880
Navalny started out writing political commentary online. 

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.240
That’s why Putin’s people dismiss him as the blogger.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.080
But this is what he’s known for.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:42.720
They’re slick, investigative videos about corruption among Russia’s rich and powerful. They get millions of views.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.280
What hasn’t stagnated is Navalny’s political ambition.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:23.000
He’s organised protests, run for mayor of Moscow and even tried running for president.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:27.520
But it’s been a tough fight.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.360
Navalny and his supporters say he’s been held back at every turn.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:35.920
Multiple arrests for protesting and two convictions for embezzlement and fraud.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:41.680
Though Navalny says he’s innocent and the European Court of Human Rights had issues with the legal process.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.560
One of those convictions even got him banned from running for president in 2017.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.360
And then there were all the attacks. Antiseptic was thrown in his face. He says he was poisoned in jail.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.680
And last year someone apparently tried to kill him again.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:02.880
Navalny got really sick on a domestic flight, fell into a coma and had to be transferred to a hospital in Germany.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.600
Investigators in Berlin said he’d been poisoned with a nerve agent belonging to the Novichok family.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:16.640
It’s a Soviet-era chemical that Russian agents have been accused of using before on one of their former spies and his daughter in the UK.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.800
Alexey Navalny blamed Putin and Russian intelligence. Putin just laughed it off.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:28.400
So he's forced to go to Germany to get treatment.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.160
But he’s still on probation for one of his convictions.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:37.400
Then the Russian authorities turned around and said Navalny broke the rules by leaving the country, which means jail time.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.560
But that didn’t stop Navalny from coming home.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:03.640
So why did he return to Russia? Well Navalny says it was about sending a message, that he’s not afraid of Putin.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:25.240
For his return to Mother Russia Navalny had something special in mind — another video, this time a straight shot at Putin.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:38.280
It’s about a seaside palace that was supposedly built for Putin though a Russian billionaire who’s close to the president recently said it’s his.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:56.560
That video and Navalny’s return kicked off some of the biggest protests Russia’s had in a long time.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:05.040
They even spread to rural parts of Russia that haven’t really had big demonstrations against Putin.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:25.280
Thousands of people have been arrested, Navalny’s wife, his brother and other opposition figures.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:30.000
All of this matters because there are parliamentary elections coming up in September.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.000
Some analysts talk about Russia’s political scene being roughly divided into thirds.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.800
One-third of people who support Putin, a third who oppose him and another third who aren’t sure.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.160
Putin’s supporters like the way things are.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:51.480
They even voted for a constitutional change that lets Putin run for president two more times, meaning he could stay in power until 2036.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:59.280
And many of them don’t like Navalny. They echo what the Kremlin and state media say that he basically works for the CIA.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:13.080
There are other people who support Navalny or just want to vote for someone who isn’t allied with Putin.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.520
But what about the millions of people in this section here.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.240
Let’s be clear. Putin still has a lot of support.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:35.240
But where Navalny has the edge is with young people who use social media. It’s a weapon he understands better than most.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:38.680
And Navalny’s been campaigning to knock Putin’s base for a while.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:41.920
His project called Smart Voting kind of worked.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:45.440
United Russia lost its majority in three city councils.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:02.600
So will Navalny’s game plan work? Well, usually you’d measure that at the ballot box.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:08.800
But Russia doesn’t have the best record of free and fair elections, based on an OSCE report at least.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:25.320
Navalny’s not trying to get rid of Putin overnight but he is forcing Putin’s hand.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:31.320
Throwing Navalny in jail, arresting people, cracking down on dissent — it might just backfire.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:42.640
There’s obviously a lot more to this story and stuff is happening in Russia all the time so check our website aljazeera.com where our colleagues have lots of analysis and all the updates. 

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:44.320
I’ll see you next week.

